109 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H2
/P 68 0 R
/T (The first time I used keybr was around August of 2020 and I practiced around an average of 30 to 60 minutes. I think now that was overpracticing, but I guess I was just desperate to improve and enjoyed the process. After being somewhat consistent for 2 weeks, I went from averaging about 40 WPM to 70 WPM. That was really a big jump for me that time. For some reason, though, I didn't practice consistently for months until nearly a year later. I still improved quite a bit, however \(averaged ~90 WPM\). Eventually I was able to break the 100 WPM border around mid of 2021, and that was also when I discovered monkeytype.com. I thought monkeytype was really cool and liked the minimalistic design, so I eventually switched to it. Although I still mained keybr until switching completely to monkeytype \(around the end of 2022\). After that I was pretty satisfied being able to average more than 100 WPM, around 120-130 WPM \(in monkeytype 60s\).)
/E (The first time I used keybr was around August of 2020 and I practiced around an average of 30 to 60 minutes. I think now that was overpracticing, but I guess I was just desperate to improve and enjoyed the process. After being somewhat consistent for 2 weeks, I went from averaging about 40 WPM to 70 WPM. That was really a big jump for me that time. For some reason, though, I didn't practice consistently for months until nearly a year later. I still improved quite a bit, however \(averaged ~90 WPM\). Eventually I was able to break the 100 WPM border around mid of 2021, and that was also when I discovered monkeytype.com. I thought monkeytype was really cool and liked the minimalistic design, so I eventually switched to it. Although I still mained keybr until switching completely to monkeytype \(around the end of 2022\). After that I was pretty satisfied being able to average more than 100 WPM, around 120-130 WPM \(in monkeytype 60s\).)
/Pg 12 0 R
/K [1 2]
>>
endobj
